data.content:
**What changed:** `TimeProvider` is injected via constructor with a default of `TimeProvider.System`, so existing callers are unaffected (no DI container needed).

**In tests** (add the `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` NuGet package):

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 12, 31, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));

fake.SetUtcNow(new DateTimeOffset(2024, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 12, 31, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

This is the lightest path because `TimeProvider` ships in the base class library (no new abstractions to define) and the optional parameter means zero changes to production call sites.
